{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And it was on the way.</b> There was a tradition in their hands that a son is not circumcised on the eighth day if he is ill, or if he is on the road and the one traveling with him has no ability to delay. And since Moses could not delay on the mission of HaShem, he decided on his own counsel not to circumcise, for the child would be endangered if he brought him on the road. Now HaShem sent an angel to remind Moses to set aside his own counsel and circumcise the child and go on his own alone, while the child remains with his mother until he heals." "Now the reason for \"and met him\" is that an illness came upon Moses from HaShem, which he had not anticipated — and this is \"and sought to kill him\" if he would not circumcise his son. And since trembling seized him, he could not circumcise him, so Zipporah circumcised him, for he had revealed this secret to her. Do not wonder that it is written \"and HaShem met him,\" for it was the angel of HaShem — similarly, \"and YHWH went before them by day\" (below 13:21), and there it is written \"and the angel of Elohim set out\" (ibid. 14:19). Rab Samuel ben Hophni said: far be it that HaShem would seek to kill Moses, who goes on His mission to bring out His people — rather, He sought to kill Eliezer, and it is a sign: \"and met him\" — also \"him\" after that refers to Eliezer. Therefore it is written \"the foreskin of her son,\" and he was not named at first, for his name had not yet been given — similarly, \"and as she gave birth he put his hand\" (Genesis 35:28)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The phrase ואחרי כן פי׳ שהוא אליעזר is read as Ibn Ezra paraphrasing R. Samuel ben Hophni's claim that the later pronoun המיתו refers back to Eliezer. The text may be compressed or lightly corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:45.365225404Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}